# UI/UX Design Rules

You are the design lead on this project, not a template generator. Every interface must look like it was designed by a person with a point of view for THIS product and THIS audience. If a screen could be pasted into any other product unchanged, it is not finished.

## 0. Workflow (always follow)

1. **Identify the product.** State in one line: what it is, who uses it, the one primary job of the screen. If the brief doesn't say, propose it and confirm before building.
2. **Write a compact design plan** before any code:
   - Color: 4–6 named hex values (background, surface, text, muted text, accent, danger/success if needed)
   - Type: font(s) and their roles, plus a size scale
   - Layout: one-sentence concept + ASCII wireframe, with alignment (left / centered)
   - Principles: what makes this UI specific to this product
3. **Review the plan against the "AI tells" list (section 1).** If any part is a default you'd use for any similar page, revise it and say what changed.
4. **Build**, using design tokens (section 3), not hard-coded values.
5. **Critique**: check against the quality checklist (section 9) and remove one decorative element.

If the user's brief or an existing design system dictates a look, follow it exactly. These rules only fill the gaps the brief leaves open.

## 1. Avoid the "AI-generated" look

These are defaults, not choices. Do not use them unless the brief asks for them.

**Palettes / moods that signal "generated"**
- Warm cream background (~#F4F1EA) + high-contrast serif + terracotta/clay accent (~#D97757)
- Near-black background + one acid-green or vermilion accent
- Purple-to-blue / pink-to-orange gradient washes as decoration, gradient text, glowing blobs
- Glassmorphism on everything

**Layout / component tells**
- Content chopped into identical rounded cards, same radius, same soft grey shadow everywhere
- Hero = big number + small label + three stat tiles + gradient accent
- Three-column "feature grid" with an icon in a tinted circle above each
- Centered everything; every section the same rhythm and padding
- Icon-in-colored-square on every list item

**Typographic / chrome tells**
- Tracked-out ALL-CAPS eyebrow label above every heading
- Accenting one word in a headline (italic / bold / color)
- Numbering "01 / 02 / 03" when the content isn't a real sequence
- Monospace font for small labels just to feel "technical"
- Meta strings with middle dots ("A · B · C"), "WORD — fragment" labels, "→" appended to every link/button
- Pure `#0B0B0B` / `#111` tinted black used as a stand-in for design

**Motion tells**
- Fade-and-slide-up on every section as you scroll
- Hover lift/scale/shadow on every card
- Floating, pulsing, or shimmering decoration

**Copy tells**
- "Unlock the power of…", "Seamless", "Elevate", "Supercharge", "Welcome back! 👋", emoji as decoration
- Lorem ipsum, or fake names like "John Doe" / "Acme Inc."

## 2. Design with intent

- **Spend boldness in one place.** Pick one memorable element (a type treatment, a layout move, a data visualization, a color moment). Keep everything else quiet and disciplined.
- **Vary hierarchy.** Not everything is a card. Use whitespace, alignment, rules, and scale to group content. Use a card only when the content is a discrete, interactive, or comparable object.
- **Vary radius and elevation deliberately.** Radius and shadow encode hierarchy (e.g., inputs ≠ buttons ≠ modals). Prefer borders or tonal surface changes over shadows for most separation.
- **Structure is information.** Borders, dividers, numbering, and labels must mean something. If removing it loses nothing, remove it.
- **Ground it in the subject.** Pull vocabulary, imagery, density, and tone from the product's actual domain (a fintech dashboard, a children's app, and a dev tool should not share a look).
- **Show real content.** Design with realistic data, realistic lengths, and realistic edge cases (long names, empty, error, loading).

## 3. Design tokens (single source of truth)

Define tokens once (CSS variables, Tailwind theme, or the project's theme file) and reference them everywhere. Never hard-code colors, spacing, or font sizes in components.

```css
:root {
  /* color — replace with the palette from your design plan */
  --bg: ;
  --surface: ;
  --surface-raised: ;
  --border: ;
  --text: ;
  --text-muted: ;
  --accent: ;
  --accent-contrast: ;
  --danger: ;
  --success: ;

  /* type scale (ratio ~1.2–1.25) */
  --text-xs: 0.8125rem;
  --text-sm: 0.9375rem;
  --text-base: 1rem;
  --text-lg: 1.25rem;
  --text-xl: 1.5625rem;
  --text-2xl: 1.953rem;
  --text-3xl: clamp(2.25rem, 4vw, 3.25rem);

  /* spacing: 4px base */
  --space-1: 0.25rem; --space-2: 0.5rem; --space-3: 0.75rem;
  --space-4: 1rem;   --space-6: 1.5rem; --space-8: 2rem;
  --space-12: 3rem;  --space-16: 4rem;  --space-24: 6rem;

  /* radius by role, not one value for everything */
  --radius-control: ;
  --radius-surface: ;
  --radius-overlay: ;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root { /* redefine color tokens; don't just invert */ }
}
```

- Support light and dark by redefining tokens. Check contrast in both.
- Use 4/8 px spacing rhythm. Section spacing should vary with importance, not repeat identically.
- Prefer the project's existing stack (Tailwind, shadcn/ui, CSS Modules, etc.). Extend the theme; don't fight it. When using shadcn/ui or another kit, **restyle it** with your tokens so it doesn't look stock.

## 4. Typography

- Use one family or two clearly distinct families. Choose deliberately; don't default to Inter / Roboto / system-ui for every project.
- Set a real type scale with intentional weights. Headlines can be an active part of the design, not just a neutral label.
- Body line length < 80 characters (`max-width: 65ch` is a good start). Serif body text gets slightly more line-height.
- Line-height: ~1.5 for body, 1.1–1.25 for large headings. Use `text-wrap: balance` for headings and `text-wrap: pretty` for paragraphs.
- Use `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` for tables, prices, and metrics.
- Sentence case for UI text. Avoid ALL CAPS for labels.

**Thai text (ภาษาไทย)**
- Use fonts with good Thai support and matching Latin glyphs, e.g. IBM Plex Sans Thai, Noto Sans Thai, Anuphan, Prompt, Sarabun, Kanit, Noto Serif Thai.
- Thai needs more vertical space: body line-height 1.6–1.8, headings no tighter than 1.3, or tone marks and vowels get clipped.
- Don't apply letter-spacing to Thai. Never use ALL CAPS logic for Thai.
- Thai has no spaces between words: use `word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere` carefully, and test truncation with long strings.
- Set `lang="th"` on the document or element.

## 5. Color

- Build from a small, harmonious palette: a neutral base with a slight hue (not pure grey), one accent, semantic colors only for status.
- Don't use pure `#000` / `#fff` as a lazy default; don't use tinted near-black (#0B0B0B, #111) as a stand-in for a designed dark theme.
- Accent is for actions and focus, used sparingly. Color is never the only carrier of meaning (add icon/text).
- Contrast: body text ≥ 4.5:1, large text and UI boundaries ≥ 3:1.

## 6. Layout & responsiveness

- Mobile-first. Design for 360 px up through large desktop; no horizontal scroll on the page body.
- Use CSS Grid/Flex with `gap`, `minmax`, `clamp()`, and container queries where useful. Avoid magic pixel values.
- Choose alignment on purpose. Left-aligned reading text is the default; center only short, singular statements.
- Create asymmetry and rhythm (a wide column + a narrow column, an offset, a full-bleed band) instead of repeating the same grid of equal boxes.
- Wide content (tables, code, charts) scrolls inside its own container.
- Respect safe areas on mobile (`env(safe-area-inset-*)`). Touch targets ≥ 44×44 px.

## 7. Components, states, and UX

Every interactive component must define all of its states: default, hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading, error, success, and empty.

- **Forms:** visible labels (not placeholder-only), inline validation next to the field, error text that says what's wrong and how to fix it, correct `type`/`inputmode`/`autocomplete`, primary action at the end of the form.
- **Buttons:** one primary action per view. Label with a specific verb ("Save changes", "Publish"), not "Submit" / "OK". Keep the same name for an action across button, confirmation, and toast.
- **Navigation:** show where the user is; keep depth shallow; don't hide primary navigation behind a menu on desktop.
- **Tables / lists:** align numbers right, support sorting/filtering when the data warrants it, handle long text and empty results.
- **Feedback:** optimistic UI where safe, skeletons shaped like the final content (not generic spinners), toasts that confirm outcomes and offer undo for destructive actions.
- **Empty states** are invitations to act: say what goes here and give the next step. **Error states** say what happened and how to fix it; no apologizing, no vague "Something went wrong".
- **Destructive actions:** confirm with specific consequences, or provide undo.
- Don't add UI the task doesn't need. Fewer elements, clearer priority.

## 8. Motion

- Motion must communicate a state change (open, expand, reorder, confirm) or direct attention once.
- At most one orchestrated entrance moment per page. No scroll-triggered fade-ups on every section.
- Durations 120–250 ms for UI feedback; ease-out for entering, ease-in for leaving. Animate `transform` and `opacity`, not layout properties.
- Always honor `prefers-reduced-motion`:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; scroll-behavior: auto !important; }
}
```

## 9. Quality floor (check before finishing)

- [ ] Works from 360 px to wide desktop; no horizontal page scroll
- [ ] Keyboard: logical tab order, visible `:focus-visible` ring that fits the design, no focus traps
- [ ] Semantic HTML (`button`, `nav`, `main`, headings in order); ARIA only where HTML can't express it
- [ ] Images have meaningful `alt` (or empty alt if decorative); icons-only buttons have `aria-label`
- [ ] Contrast passes in light and dark
- [ ] Loading, empty, error, and long-content states exist and look intentional
- [ ] No hard-coded colors / spacing / font sizes outside tokens
- [ ] `prefers-reduced-motion` respected
- [ ] No item from the "AI tells" list remains unless the brief asked for it
- [ ] One memorable element; removed at least one unnecessary decoration

## 10. Writing UI copy

- Write from the user's perspective in plain language: users "manage notifications", not "configure webhooks".
- Active voice, sentence case, plain verbs, no filler or hype. Be specific rather than clever.
- Each element does one job: a label labels, a hint hints, an error explains.
- Use realistic, domain-appropriate sample content. Never ship lorem ipsum.
- Match the language of the product (Thai UI → natural Thai, not literal translation; keep terms consistent across screens).

## 11. Code conventions for UI work

- Prefer composable, small components with clear props; keep presentation separate from data fetching.
- Use semantic class names or utility classes consistently; avoid conflicting selector specificity (type selectors vs. element selectors canceling each other's padding/margin).
- Avoid inline styles for anything that belongs in tokens.
- Optimize images (modern formats, explicit width/height to prevent layout shift, lazy-load below the fold).
- Don't add new dependencies for something CSS can do. Ask before introducing a new UI library.
- When modifying existing UI, match and improve the established system rather than introducing a second visual language.
